  owner: pm-gh1581
body: "Follow-up to #946.\n\n## Observed\n\nWhen another process has changed items since the last read, `pm context` rebuilds its persisted context-signal snapshot and returns correct, fresh output. It still emits:\n\n```\nwarning_details[1]{code,meaning,recovery_command,recovery_effect}:\n  context_signal_store_stale,The persisted context-signal snapshot did not match the authoritative cursor or item corpus and was rebuilt.,pm context,Re-read context and confirm the rebuilt snapshot is now fresh.\nwarnings[1]: context_signal_store_stale\n```\n\nThe recovery advice asks the agent to run `pm context` again. That second read returns the same content without the warning: 2.4–3.7 KB (~600–940 estimated tokens) spent only to clear a cache message. In a multi-agent tracker, where other writers change items between every read, this happens on most first reads of a session. Seen on 2026-10-25 in two independent trackers (a companion hub with 1,285 items and a package tracker), each time followed by a clean re-read.\n\n## Expected\n\nA cache that was detected stale and rebuilt successfully within the same call is not a condition the caller needs to act on. Either:\n\n- report it as informational metadata (for example `cache: { context_signals: \"rebuilt\" }` in the read receipt) instead of `warnings`, with no recovery command; or\n- keep a warning only when the rebuild failed or the output was produced from the stale snapshot.\n\nAgents treat `warnings` as something to act on, so a self-healed cache should not appear there.\n\nVersion: `@unbrained/pm-cli` 2026.10.24."
